===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|en|foot-ball|foot ball||dated}}

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*ped-|*bʰel-|id2=blow}}
From {{inh|en|enm|fotbal}}, {{m|enm|footbal}}, equivalent to {{com|en|foot|ball}}, which may refer to the act of kicking a ball with the feet or to the fact the game was played on foot (as opposed to on [[horseback]] or with players in fixed positions). The name for the briefcase is a play on “dropkick”, the code name of an early version of the nuclear war plan.<ref>{{cite-web
|title=Military aides still carry the president's nuclear 'football'
|work=USA Today
|date=2005-05-05
|author=Associated Press
|titleurl=https://usatoday30.usatoday.com/news/washington/2005-05-05-nuclear-football_x.htm
|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20150226002251/https://usatoday30.usatoday.com/news/washington/2005-05-05-nuclear-football_x.htm
|archivedate=2015-02-26
|passage=It got its nickname because an early version of the nuclear war plan — the SIOP, or Single Integrated Operational Plan — was code-named "dropkick."}}</ref>

